cetrafs licensing
=================

cetrafs is split across two licenses, because its two halves have
genuinely different constraints. Every file carries an
`SPDX-License-Identifier:` line; that line, not this summary, is
authoritative for that file.

    kmod/             GPL-2.0-or-later
    include/          GPL-2.0-or-later OR AGPL-3.0-or-later
                        OR LicenseRef-cetrafs-Proprietary
    userspace/        AGPL-3.0-or-later OR LicenseRef-cetrafs-Proprietary
    tests/, ctest/    GPL-2.0-or-later
                      (except two fuzz helpers that build against
                      userspace headers, which follow userspace)

Everything else in the repository -- documentation, packaging, build
files -- is GPL-2.0-or-later.

Full texts are in LICENSES/.

The kernel module: GPL-2.0-or-later
-----------------------------------

`kmod/` is a Linux kernel module. It is derived from and links against
the kernel, so it is GPL-2.0-or-later and is NOT dual-licensed -- there
is no proprietary option for it and none can be offered. It declares
`MODULE_LICENSE("GPL")` accordingly.

The userspace tools: AGPL-3.0-or-later, or commercial
-----------------------------------------------------

`userspace/` (mkfs.cetra, fsck.cetra, defrag.cetra) is dual-licensed:
AGPL-3.0-or-later for open use, or a commercial license from the
copyright holder for those whom the AGPL's source-availability
obligation does not suit. See LICENSES/LicenseRef-cetrafs-Proprietary.txt.

The shared on-disk-format header
--------------------------------

`include/cetrafs.h` is the single source of truth for the on-disk format
and is compiled verbatim into BOTH halves. It is therefore offered under
any of the three licenses above, so that each half can take it under
terms it can actually use: the module takes it as GPL-2.0-or-later, the
open tools as AGPL-3.0-or-later, and a commercial licensee under the
commercial terms. A recipient chooses one.

This also means the on-disk format itself is documented and implementable
by anyone: see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. An independent reader of a cetrafs
volume is not encumbered by the choice of license here.

